<article class="env-remark" id="05EV">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/05EV">Remark <span data-tag="05EV">15.91.21</span>.</a> Given a differential manifold $X$ with a compact closed submanifold $Z$ having complement $U$, specifying a sheaf on $X$ is the same as specifying a sheaf on $U$, a sheaf on an unspecified tubular neighbourhood $T$ of $Z$ in $X$, and an isomorphism between the two resulting sheaves along $T \cap U$. Tubular neighbourhoods do not exist in algebraic geometry as such, but results such as Proposition <a href="/tag/05ER" data-tag="05ER">15.91.16</a>, Theorem <a href="/tag/05ES" data-tag="05ES">15.91.18</a>, and Proposition <a href="/tag/05ET" data-tag="05ET">15.91.19</a> allow us to work with formal neighbourhoods instead. </p>
</article>